WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.761
Before installing software or ordering upgrades, create an accurate hardware inventory.

00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:09.100
Record the exact GPU model and its usable VRAM, not just the product family.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.660
VRAM often defines the largest model that can remain GPU-resident,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.080
while GPU generation influences supported data types and acceleration features.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.440
Note the CPU model, generation, physical cores,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.840
and instruction support because model loading, prompt processing,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.533
and CPU fallback can place significant demand on it.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:29.060
Record installed system RAM, available memory slots, current module arrangement,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.975
and the motherboard maximum.

00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:34.095
System RAM must support the operating system, runtime, model loading,

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:36.935
and any portion of the model that spills beyond VRAM.

00:00:36.935 --> 00:00:41.360
Inventory each SSD by interface, capacity, free space, and approximate speed.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.820
Model collections grow quickly,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.920
and insufficient free space can turn routine model updates into a maintenance problem.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.240
Inspect the motherboard for available PCI Express slots, lane sharing, physical clearance,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.310
storage connectors, and firmware support.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.370
Then verify the power supply model, rated wattage, age, connector availability,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.000
and quality.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.240
A wattage number by itself is not enough;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.010
a future GPU must be supported by the correct cables and safe sustained output.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.480
Review case dimensions, fan placement, dust buildup, and airflow from intake to exhaust.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.310
AI inference can hold components under load longer than a short gaming burst,

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.235
revealing thermal limits that casual use never exposed.

00:01:15.235 --> 00:01:16.750
Finally, test the real machine.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.020
Check memory usage, temperatures, clocks, and power behavior under an existing workload.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.613
Consult manufacturer documentation before purchasing.

00:01:23.613 --> 00:01:27.200
An inventory converts vague assumptions into constraints: which models fit,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.025
which runtimes are supported, whether an upgrade is physically and electrically possible,

00:01:31.025 --> 00:01:33.180
and where money would produce a measurable improvement.
